    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `check_server(server, cases)` in `mcpcheck/inproc.py`, and the full test suite passes (4 tests, including 2 new ones).\n\n**How it works:** The installed mcp SDK (2.2.0) already has an `mcp.Client` that connects to a server object in the same process, over in-memory streams with no subprocess or sockets. `check_server` opens one of those and runs the same loop as `check_command`: each case's tool is called in order with `session.call_tool`, the result goes through the existing `compare`, and mismatches come back in the same order.\n\n- **`mode=\"legacy\"`:** by default `Client` skips the normal protocol messages and calls the server directly. This setting makes it do the same startup handshake (`initialize`) that `check_command` does over stdio, so results should match what stdio would give.\n- **`cache=None`:** turns off the client's response caching. `Client` has it on by default and it's only used for list calls, not tool calls, so this is just a safeguard.\n- **Accepted servers:** servers built with the decorator API (`MCPServer` with `@server.tool()`) and low-level `Server` objects both work, because `Client` handles both.\n\nNo dependencies were added. `anyio`, used in the tests, already comes with the SDK.\n\n**Tests:** `tests/test_inproc.py` builds a server with the decorator API. One test runs `examples/cases.toml` against it and expects no mismatches. The other checks that several mismatches are reported in case order with the text the server actually returned.\n\nA server author can use it in their own tests like this:\n```python\nassert anyio.run(check_server, my_server, load_cases(\"cases.toml\")) == []\n```"
  },
